# `kk`

`kk` performs the real principal-value Kramers-Kronig transform of a tabulated
function. If $S(E)$ is the selected interpolant on $[a,b]$, the reported value
at an interior point is

$$
K(z)=\frac{1}{\pi}\operatorname{PV}\int_a^b\frac{S(E)}{E-z}\,dE.
$$

## Usage

```text
kk [options] input output
kk [options] -
```

The second form reads two-column `x y` data from standard input and writes only
numeric result rows to standard output. Diagnostics and errors are written to
standard error.

Input is line-oriented. Blank lines and full-line comments whose first
non-whitespace character is `#` are skipped. Every other physical line must
contain exactly two finite representable numbers. Inline comments, partial or
extra records, malformed fields, and values that overflow or underflow to zero
are errors reported with the source and line number.

Input is sorted by `x`. The coordinates must then be strictly increasing and
their total span must be finite. The input must contain an even number of
points on a grid symmetric around zero. The result is evaluated on that grid.

In file mode, input and output must not be the same filesystem object,
including through a symbolic or hard link. Parsing and transformation finish
before the output is opened, so an existing output survives those failures.
Output open, write, flush, and close failures are fatal. Writing is not an
atomic replacement: a failure after the destination is opened can leave it
truncated or incomplete.

## Options

| Option | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `-h`, `--help` | Print help. |
| `-a ALGORITHM`, `--algorithm ALGORITHM` | Select `qag` or `analytic`. Default: `qag`. |
| `-i METHOD`, `--interpolation METHOD` | Select `linear`, `cspline`, `akima`, or `steffen`. Default: `akima`. |
| `-j N`, `--jobs N` | Request `N` positive worker threads. The default is the first `OMP_NUM_THREADS` value, or one when the variable is unset. |
| `--epsabs VALUE` | Set the QAG absolute tolerance. Default: `1e-12`. |
| `--epsrel VALUE` | Set the QAG relative tolerance. Default: `1e-8`. |
| `--workspace-limit N` | Set each QAG worker's positive workspace capacity. Default: `1000`. |
| `--quadrature-rule RULE` | Select QAG rule `15`, `21`, `31`, `41`, `51`, or `61`. Default: `15`. |
| `--gsl-error-policy POLICY` | Handle QAG failures with `ignore`, `warn`, or `fail`. Default: `ignore`. |
| `-v` | Print the resolved configuration to standard error. |
| `-vv` | Increase verbosity further. |
| `-V`, `--version` | Print the project version. |

`linear` is piecewise linear and requires at least two points. `cspline` is a
natural cubic spline and requires at least three points. `akima` is a local
piecewise-cubic interpolant and requires at least five points. `steffen` is a
monotonicity-preserving piecewise-cubic interpolant and requires at least three
points. `cspline` and `akima` can overshoot between samples. `steffen` stays
within each interval's endpoint range, but its second derivative can be
discontinuous and its shape can be conservative near extrema.

The QAG controls apply to the default algorithm. Specifying them together with
`--algorithm analytic` is an error.

## QAG Algorithm (Default)

The default algorithm is the historical adaptive Gauss-Kronrod calculation.
For each nonnegative integration coordinate it combines the positive and
negative interpolant contributions before integration. QAG arguments must lie
inside the interpolation domain; command-line operation satisfies this because
output is evaluated on the input grid.

QAG is generally the faster choice for large smooth tables, but its result has
adaptive-quadrature error. Select the analytic algorithm when its stronger
accuracy and extreme-range guarantees are required.

The workspace limit applies independently to every worker. A failed QAG target
is ignored, reported, or made fatal according to `--gsl-error-policy`.
Nonfinite interpolation, integration, correction, or output values are always
fatal under every policy.

## Analytic Algorithm

With `--algorithm analytic`, the selected GSL interpolant is materialized once
as a polynomial on each input interval: degree one for `linear` and degree three
for the other methods. `kk` integrates each polynomial divided by $E-z$
analytically using polynomial division and logarithms, then combines the
interval contributions with compensated summation. It does not use adaptive
quadrature, so QAG controls are not accepted in this mode. A guarded real-valued
far-interval path uses direct extended-precision error bounds for ordinary
finite data. If cancellation, overflow, underflow, or convergence cannot be
certified, that interval is restarted with the logarithmic, wider, and
exact-arithmetic implementation.

## Parallel Execution

Output points are independent. `--jobs N` requests up to `N` workers while
preserving input order and per-point numerical operation order. The actual
worker count is the smaller of the requested count and the number of output
points. Each QAG worker owns a separate GSL interpolation accelerator and
integration workspace.

When `--jobs` is omitted, `kk` uses the first positive integer in
`OMP_NUM_THREADS`. Thus `OMP_NUM_THREADS=8` and `OMP_NUM_THREADS=8,4` both select
eight workers. An explicit `--jobs` value overrides the environment. If the
variable is unset, one worker is used. Invalid and nonpositive values are
errors. The `-v` configuration report shows the resolved requested count and
its source; the file-mode performance line shows the actual worker count.

## Timing

After a successful file-mode run, `kk` reports total wall time on standard
output:

```text
Time elapsed: 1.234 s
```

The time includes command parsing, input, interpolant setup, transformation,
and output-file writing. With `-v`, a second line reports process CPU time,
effective parallelism (`CPU time / wall time`), total throughput, actual worker
count, and algorithm. Effective parallelism describes observed CPU use; it is
not a measured speedup against a separate serial run. If process CPU time is
unavailable, the fields are reported as `cpu=n/a effective_parallelism=n/a`.

Stream mode (`kk -`) emits no timing report and keeps standard output strictly
numeric. Library construction and calls are likewise silent.

At $z=a$ or $z=b$, the ordinary one-sided transform generally has a logarithmic
divergence. For compatibility, `kk` reports the finite endpoint-subtracted
value

$$
K_{\mathrm{sub}}(z)=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_a^b
\frac{S(E)-S(z)}{E-z}\,dE.
$$

With the analytic algorithm, the displayed `Sum` is evaluated from the interval
polynomials. With QAG it is evaluated by GSL from the selected spline.
